.TH KIRISUTE 1 "2026-11-02" "kirisute" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kirisute \- cut GBA, DS, DSi and 3DS ROMs and PSP ISOs to their real size
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kirisute
.br
.B kirisute
.RI [ options ] " " rom ...
.br
.B kirisute rename
.RI [ options ] " " rom ...
.br
.B kirisute compress
.RI [ options ] " " iso ...
.br
.B kirisute doctor
.br
.B kirisute version
.br
.B kirisute help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kirisute
removes the padding at the end of Game Boy Advance, Nintendo DS, DSi and 3DS
ROM files and PSP disc images.
.PP
A DS ROM is cut to the size its header declares: the used ROM size, plus its
download-play RSA certificate when it has one, and the full DSi area for a
DSi-enhanced or DSi-only ROM.
.PP
A Game Boy Advance ROM declares no size, so it is cut after its last byte that
is not padding, when its final byte is 00 or FF. The cut is rounded up to a
4-byte boundary, and a ROM that would lose less than 512 bytes stays as it is.
.PP
A 3DS cartridge image is cut at the end of its last partition, counted in the
media unit its header sets. It must carry the NCSD magic and a media ID, have a
first partition, keep its partitions in order without overlap, and keep them
within its declared size; a console NAND image is refused. A 3DS executable
carrying the NCCH magic is cut to the size its header declares.
.PP
A PSP ISO is cut to the volume its ISO 9660 primary volume descriptor declares,
the volume space size times the logical block size. Its descriptor must name
the system PSP GAME, UMD VIDEO or UMD AUDIO, or its root must hold a
.I PSP_GAME
folder; its title and disc ID come from
.IR PSP_GAME/PARAM.SFO ,
when it has one. A dump can hold sectors past the declared volume, and a game
can read sectors outside its files, so the cut is made only when every byte
past the volume is 00 or FF, with or without
.BR \-\-verify .
Nothing inside the volume is removed, update data and padding files included. A
development DVD-R image holding
.I USER_L0.IMG
and
.I USER_L1.IMG
is refused until the two layers are joined, layer 0 first.
.PP
A trimmed file no longer matches the checksum of an untrimmed dump in
databases such as Redump or No-Intro;
.B \-\-backup
keeps the original.
.PP
A file given by name is read by its extension:
.I .gba
for Game Boy Advance,
.I .nds
for DS and DSi,
.I .3ds
or
.I .cci
for a 3DS cartridge image and
.I .cxi
for a 3DS executable and
.I .iso
for a PSP disc image, and as a DS ROM when it has none of these. A folder gives
those files directly
inside it, and every subfolder too with
.BR \-\-recursive .
Symbolic links, hidden files and hidden folders are left alone, and
.B kirisute
looks no deeper than 32 folders.
.PP
A file whose header fails its checks, that is too small to hold a header, or
whose header declares more than the file holds, stays as it is with a warning.
Each trimmed ROM is written to a temporary file beside it, given the original's
permissions and renamed over it, so an interrupted run never leaves half a
file.
.PP
Before a trim,
.B kirisute
prints how many ROMs it found and what it is about to do. Without a ROM on a
terminal it asks for a file or folder, offering the current folder.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B rename
Rename each ROM after the title and game code in its header, as
.IR "KIRISUTE (KSTE).nds" ,
or after the code alone when the header has no title, as a 3DS image does.
The extension is kept in lowercase, slashes and colons in a title become
hyphens, and control characters are dropped. A ROM already named that way is
left alone, and a name another file already has is never taken. It takes
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.B \-\-recursive
and
.BR \-\-json ,
which reports a
.I renamed
count and each file's
.IR new_name .
.TP
.B compress
Write a compressed copy of each PSP disc image beside it with maxcso, in a
format ARK-4 and ARK-5 read:
.I game.cso
for CSO v1 or CSO v2, or
.I game.zso
for ZSO. The original is kept. A copy is kept only after it decompresses back to
the original byte for byte, which needs free space as large as the image while
the check runs; otherwise it is deleted. An existing copy is never overwritten,
and a file that is not a PSP disc image is not compressed. The defaults give the
same bytes as a CSO v2 that boots on ARK-4 and ARK-5: 8 KiB blocks, zlib, 7-zip
deflate, LZ4 and brute-force LZ4, with LZ4 only where it is smaller. DAX is not
written,
because maxcso's DAX blocks lack the zlib header ARK expects, and JSO is not
written, because no open JSO writer exists. It takes
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.BR \-\-recursive ,
.B \-\-json
and the options under COMPRESS.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools kirisute needs, with the flavor and version of each, maxcso
included. Exits non-zero only when one is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
List what would be trimmed and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-info
Print each ROM's header and change nothing: its title and game code when it has
them, then the unit code and download-play certificate of a DS ROM, the maker
code and revision of a Game Boy Advance ROM, the media ID and partition count
of a 3DS cartridge image, the program ID of a 3DS executable or the volume
identifier of a PSP ISO, then its trimmed size and padding.
.TP
.BR \-\-verify ", " \-\-no\-verify
Trim only a ROM whose padding holds nothing but 00 or FF bytes. A ROM with any
other byte past its real size stays as it is, and the warning names the first
such byte.
.TP
.BR \-\-backup ", " \-\-no\-backup
Keep each original as
.IR rom .bak.
A ROM that already has one stays as it is.
.TP
.B \-\-recursive
Look for ROM files in every subfolder of each folder given.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON object on stdout, for scripts: the counts of ROMs
found, read, trimmed, already trimmed, skipped and failed, the bytes saved, and
one entry per file with its status, size, kind and trimmed size.
.PP
.B \-\-
ends the options. A file named help, doctor, version, rename or compress is
trimmed by giving its path, such as
.IR ./help .
Every option that takes a value also takes the form
.IR \-\-name = value .
.SH COMPRESS
.TP
.BI \-\-format " cso|cso2|zso"
CSO v1 is deflate only; CSO v2 picks deflate or LZ4 for each block; ZSO is LZ4
only and reads fastest. Default cso2.
.TP
.BI \-\-block " 2048|4096|8192|16384"
Block size in bytes. Larger blocks compress a little better and cost the
firmware more memory per open image. Default 8192.
.TP
.BI \-\-lz4\-cost " N"
For CSO v2, let a block use LZ4 when that costs at most N percent more space
than deflate, from 0 to 100, trading space for read speed. Default 0, LZ4 only
where it is smaller, as in the copies tested on ARK-4 and ARK-5; a higher value
makes more LZ4 blocks, which have not been tested on the console.
.TP
.B \-\-best
Add one more slow trial: Zopfli deflate for CSO v1 and v2, brute-force LZ4 for
ZSO. A slightly smaller copy, made much more slowly.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KIRISUTE_VERIFY
yes or no, the default for
.BR \-\-verify ,
no unless set.
.TP
.B KIRISUTE_BACKUP
yes or no, the default for
.BR \-\-backup ,
no unless set.
.TP
.B KIRISUTE_FORMAT
The default for
.BR \-\-format ,
cso2 unless set.
.TP
.B KIRISUTE_BLOCK
The default for
.BR \-\-block ,
8192 unless set.
.TP
.B KIRISUTE_LZ4_COST
The default for
.BR \-\-lz4\-cost ,
0 unless set.
.TP
.B KIRISUTE_BEST
yes or no, the default for
.BR \-\-best ,
no unless set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every ROM was trimmed, renamed or compressed, or already at its real size
or name. 1 when a file was skipped or could not be trimmed, renamed or
compressed, or the arguments are wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B kirisute \-\-dry\-run *.nds *.3ds
Show what trimming every ROM in the current folder would do.
.TP
.B kirisute \-\-backup \-\-verify game.nds
Trim one ROM, keep the original, and refuse if the padding holds data.
.TP
.B kirisute \-\-recursive ~/roms
Trim every ROM under a folder.
.TP
.B kirisute rename \-\-dry\-run *.gba
Show the name each Game Boy Advance ROM in the current folder would take.
.TP
.B kirisute compress game.iso
Write game.cso as CSO v2 in 8 KiB blocks, for ARK-4 and ARK-5.
.TP
.B kirisute compress \-\-format zso \-\-recursive ~/roms/psp
Write a ZSO copy of every PSP disc image under a folder.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR maxcso (1),
.BR od (1)
